positive_text:
Let $M = \{1, 2, \dots, 2013\}$ and consider a circle $\Gamma$. Assign to each subset $\mathcal{A} \subseteq M$ a unique point on $\Gamma$ such that:
1. Each subset corresponds to a distinct point;
2. All assigned points form the vertices of a regular polygon;
3. For any regular $k$-gon formed by assigned points (with $k > 2$), the sum of the subset sums of the corresponding subsets is divisible by $2014$. Is this assignment possible?

negatives.text:
Let $M = \{1, 2, \dots, 2013\}$ and consider a circle $\Gamma$. Assign each subset $\mathcal{A} \subseteq M$ to a unique point on $\Gamma$ so that:
1. Each subset corresponds to a unique point;
2. All points are vertices of a regular polygon;
3. If $k > 2$ points form a regular $k$-gon, then the total of their subset sums is divisible by $2013$. Is such an assignment possible?

Let $M = \{1, 2, \dots, 2013\}$ and consider a circle $\Gamma$. Assign each subset $\mathcal{A} \subseteq M$ to a unique point on $\Gamma$ so that:
1. Each subset corresponds to a unique point;
2. All points are vertices of a regular polygon;
3. If $k > 2$ points form a regular $k$-gon, then the product of their subset sums is divisible by $2014$. Is such an assignment possible?

Let $M = \{1, 2, \dots, 2013\}$ and consider a circle $\Gamma$. Assign each subset $\mathcal{A} \subseteq M$ to a unique point on $\Gamma$ so that:
1. Each subset corresponds to a unique point;
2. All points are vertices of a regular polygon;
3. If $k > 2$ points form a regular $k$-gon, then the total of their subset sums is congruent to $1$ modulo $2014$. Is such an assignment possible?

positive_text:
A positive integer $ n $ is called \textit{ultra special} if it can be written as $ n = \frac{a^3 + 2b^3}{c^3 + 2d^3} $ for some positive integers $ a, b, c, d $. Prove that:
(a) There are infinitely many ultra special positive integers;
(b) 2014 is not ultra special.

negatives.text:
A positive integer $ n $ is called \textit{super special} if it can be represented in the form $ n = \frac{x^3 - 2y^3}{u^3 - 2v^3} $ for some positive integers $ x, y, u, v $. Prove that:
(a) There are infinitely many super special positive integers;
(b) 2014 is not super special.

A positive integer $ n $ is called \textit{super special} if it can be represented in the form $ n = \frac{x^2 + 2y^2}{u^2 + 2v^2} $ for some positive integers $ x, y, u, v $. Prove that:
(a) There are infinitely many super special positive integers;
(b) 2014 is not super special.

A positive integer $ n $ is called \textit{super special} if it can be represented in the form $ n = \frac{x^3 + 2y^3}{u^3 - 2v^3} $ for some positive integers $ x, y, u, v $. Prove that:
(a) There are infinitely many super special positive integers;
(b) 2014 is not super special.

positive_text:
In a village with $n$ houses ($n \geqslant 3$), each house is connected to every other house by a unique road. A delivery person from each house must perform a daily round, visiting each of the other houses exactly once, using only the roads and returning to their starting house, without retracing any road used by another delivery person. 

a) Show that if $n$ is prime, the maximum number of such delivery persons is $\frac{n-1}{2}$;

b) Determine the maximum number of delivery persons if $n=9$.

negatives.text:
In a network of $n$ cities ($n \geqslant 3$), each city is connected to every other city by a single road. A tour guide from each city must perform a daily tour, visiting each of the other cities exactly once and returning to their starting city, using only the roads. No road is shared between more than one tour guide. 

a) Show that for any $n$, the maximum number of such tour guides is $\frac{n-1}{2}$;

b) Determine the maximum number of tour guides if $n=9$.

In a network of $n$ cities ($n \geqslant 3$), each city is connected to every other city by a single road. A tour guide from each city must perform a daily tour, visiting each of the other cities exactly once and returning to their starting city, using only the roads. No road is used by more than one tour guide. 

a) Show that for any prime $n$, the maximum number of such tour guides is $\frac{n}{2}$;

b) Determine the maximum number of tour guides if $n=9$.

In a network of $n$ cities ($n \geqslant 3$), each city is connected to every other city by a single road. A tour guide from each city must perform a daily tour, visiting each of the other cities exactly once and returning to their starting city, using only the roads. No road is used by more than one tour guide. 

a) Show that for any prime $n$, the maximum number of such tour guides is $\frac{n-1}{3}$;

b) Determine the maximum number of tour guides if $n=9$.

\text{In triangle } DEF \text{ with side lengths } DE = e, EF = d, FD = f, points } F_1, F_2 \text{ on } DE, D_1, D_2 \text{ on } EF, E_1, E_2 \text{ on } FD \text{ are defined so that}\\
\begin{aligned}
\frac{FD_1}{d} &= \frac{FE_2}{f} = \frac{d+f}{d+e+f}, \\
\frac{DE_1}{f} &= \frac{DF_2}{e} = \frac{e+f}{d+e+f}, \\
\frac{EF_1}{e} &= \frac{ED_2}{d} = \frac{d+e}{d+e+f}.
\end{aligned}
\text{Prove that the lines } D_1F_2, F_1E_2, E_1D_2 \text{ are concurrent at a point inside the triangle.}

positive_text:
\text{(a) Determine todos os pares }(x, y)\text{ de inteiros não negativos que satisfazem a equação}
$$
5x + 8y = 120
$$
\text{(b) Sejam }p\text{, }q\text{ e }r\text{ números inteiros positivos com }r > 1\text{ tais que}
$$
\frac{1}{p} + \frac{1}{q} = \frac{1}{r}
$$
\text{Mostre que pelo menos um dos números }p + r\text{ ou }q + r\text{ é composto}.

negatives.text:
\text{(a) Encontre todos os pares }(m, n)\text{ de inteiros positivos que satisfazem a equação}
$$
5m + 8n = 120
$$
\text{(b) Sejam }a\text{, }b\text{ e }c\text{ números inteiros positivos com }c > 1\text{ tais que}
$$
\frac{1}{a} + \frac{1}{b} = \frac{1}{c}
$$
\text{Prove que pelo menos um dos números }a + b\text{ ou }b + c\text{ é composto}.

\text{(a) Determine todos os pares }(m, n)\text{ de inteiros não negativos que satisfazem a equação}
$$
5m - 8n = 120
$$
\text{(b) Sejam }a\text{, }b\text{ e }c\text{ números inteiros positivos com }c > 1\text{ tais que}
$$
\frac{1}{a} + \frac{1}{b} = \frac{1}{c}
$$
\text{Prove que pelo menos um dos números }a + c\text{ ou }b + c\text{ é primo}.

\text{(a) Encontre todos os pares }(m, n)\text{ de inteiros não negativos que satisfazem a equação}
$$
5m + 8n = 121
$$
\text{(b) Sejam }a\text{, }b\text{ e }c\text{ números inteiros positivos com }c > 1\text{ tais que}
$$
\frac{1}{a} + \frac{1}{b} = \frac{1}{c}
$$
\text{Prove que pelo menos um dos números }a + c\text{ ou }b + c\text{ é maior que 100}.

positive_text:
Let $g(x) = 4x^{4} + 6x^{3} + 2x^{2} + 2003x - 2003^{2}$. Show that:
a) the critical points of $g'(x)$ correspond to local maxima or minima with positive values;
b) the equation $g(x) = 0$ has exactly two real solutions and determine them.

negatives.text:
Let $h(x) = 4x^{4} + 6x^{3} + 2x^{2} + 2003x + 2003^{2}$. Prove that:
a) the local extrema of $h'(x)$ are positive;
b) the equation $h(x) = 0$ has exactly two real roots and find them.

Let $p(x) = 4x^{4} + 6x^{3} + 2x^{2} + 2003x - 2003^{2}$. Prove that:
a) the local extrema of $p''(x)$ are positive;
b) the equation $p(x) = 0$ has exactly two real roots and find them.

Let $q(x) = 4x^{4} + 6x^{3} + 2x^{2} + 2003x - 2003^{2}$. Prove that:
a) the local extrema of $q'(x)$ are negative;
b) the equation $q(x) = 0$ has exactly two real roots and find them.

positive_text:
Let $X$, $Y$ and $Z$ be points on the sides $BC$, $CA$ and $AB$ of $\triangle ABC$, respectively. The lines through $X$, $Y$ and $Z$, parallel to $CA$, $AB$ and $BC$, respectively, meet at a point $Q$. Prove that:

a) if $\frac{BX}{XC} = \frac{CY}{YA} = \frac{AZ}{ZB}$, then $Q$ is the centroid of $\triangle ABC$;

b) $S_{XYZ} \leq \frac{1}{3} S_{ABC}$.

negatives.text:
Let $M$, $N$ and $P$ be points on the sides $AB$, $BC$ and $CA$ of $\triangle ABC$, respectively. The lines through $M$, $N$ and $P$, perpendicular to $BC$, $AC$ and $AB$, respectively, meet at a point $T$. Prove that:

a) if $\frac{AM}{MB} = \frac{BN}{NC} = \frac{CP}{PA}$, then $T$ is the centroid of $\triangle ABC$;

b) $S_{MNP} \leq \frac{1}{3} S_{ABC}$.

Let $M$, $N$ and $P$ be points on the sides $AB$, $BC$ and $CA$ of $\triangle ABC$, respectively. The lines through $M$, $N$ and $P$, parallel to $BC$, $AC$ and $AB$, respectively, meet at a point $T$. Prove that:

a) if $\frac{AM}{MB} = \frac{BN}{NC} = \frac{CP}{PA} = 2$, then $T$ is the centroid of $\triangle ABC$;

b) $S_{MNP} \leq \frac{1}{3} S_{ABC}$.

Let $M$, $N$ and $P$ be points on the sides $AB$, $BC$ and $CA$ of $\triangle ABC$, respectively. The lines through $M$, $N$ and $P$, parallel to $BC$, $AC$ and $AB$, respectively, meet at a point $T$. Prove that:

a) if $\frac{AM}{MB} = \frac{BN}{NC} = \frac{CP}{PA}$, then $T$ lies on the Euler line of $\triangle ABC$;

b) $S_{MNP} \leq \frac{1}{3} S_{ABC}$.

positive_text:
Let $\mathbb{R}$ be the set of real numbers. Find all $a>0$ such that there exists a function $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the following two conditions:

a) $f(x) = a x + (1 - a)$ for all $x$ in the interval $[2, 3)$;

b) $f(f(x)) = 3 - 2x$ for every $x \in \mathbb{R}$.

negatives.text:
Let $\mathbb{R}$ be the set of real numbers. Find all $a>0$ such that there exists a function $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ with the following properties:

a) $f(x) = a x + 1 - a$ for all $x \in [2, 3)$;

b) $f(f(x)) = 2x - 3$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$.

Let $\mathbb{R}$ be the set of real numbers. Find all $a>0$ such that there exists a function $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ with the following properties:

a) $f(x) = a x + 1 - a$ for all $x \in [2, 3)$;

b) $f(f(x)) = 3 - 2x$ for all $x \in [0, 1)$.

Let $\mathbb{R}$ be the set of real numbers. Find all $a>0$ such that there exists a function $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ with the following properties:

a) $f(x) = a x + 1 - a$ for all $x \in [2, 3)$;

b) $f(f(x)) = 3 - 2x^2$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$.

positive_text:
Determine all $ k > 0 $ for which there exists a function $ g : [0,1] \times [0,1] \to [0,1] $ such that the following conditions are satisfied for all $ x, y, z \in [0,1] $: 

i) $ g(g(x, y), z) = g(x, g(y, z)) $;

ii) $ g(x, y) = g(y, x) $;

iii) $ g(x, 1) = x $;

iv) $ g(z x, z y) = z^k g(x, y) $.

negatives.text:
Find all $ k > 0 $ such that there is a function $ f : [0,1] \times [0,1] \to [0,1] $ satisfying the following conditions: 

a) $ f(f(x, y), z) = f(x, f(y, z)) $;

b) $ f(x, y) = f(y, x) $;

c) $ f(x, 1) = x $;

d) $ f(z x, z y) = z^k f(x, y) $,

for any $ x, y, z \in [0,1] $, and $ f(x, y) $ is strictly increasing in both arguments.

Find all $ k > 0 $ such that there is a function $ f : [0,1] \times [0,1] \to [0,1] $ satisfying the following conditions: 

a) $ f(f(x, y), z) = f(x, f(y, z)) $;

b) $ f(x, y) = f(y, x) $;

c) $ f(x, 1) = x $;

d) $ f(z x, z y) = z^k f(x, y) $,

for any $ x, y, z \in [0,1] $, and $ f(x, y) = 0 $ when $ x = 0 $ and $ y = 0 $.

positive_text:
Consider the quadratic $g(x) = x^{2} - a x + (a^{2} - 4)$, with $a \in \mathbb{R}$. Determine all values of $a$ such that:

1) $g(x) = 0$ has two real roots $r$ and $s$ with $|r^{3} - s^{3}| \leq 4$;

2) $g(x) \geq 0$ for all integer values of $x$.

negatives.text:
Let $f(x) = x^{2} - a x + a^{2} + 4$, where $a$ is a real number. Find all $a$, for which:

a) the equation $f(x) = 0$ has two real roots $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ such that $|x_{1}^{3} - x_{2}^{3}| \leq 4$;

b) the inequality $f(x) \geq 0$ holds for all integers $x$.

Let $f(x) = x^{2} - a x + a^{2} - 4$, where $a$ is a real number. Find all $a$, for which:

a) the equation $f(x) = 0$ has two real roots $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ such that $|x_{1}^{2} - x_{2}^{2}| \leq 4$;

b) the inequality $f(x) \geq 0$ holds for all integers $x$.

positive_text:
An $n \times n$ matrix is filled with entries from the set $\{-1, 0, 1\}$. Can the $2n$ row and column sums all be distinct, if:
a) $n = 4$;
b) $n = 5$?

negatives.text:
In every cell of an $n \times n$ table one of the numbers $-1$, $0$, and $1$ is written. Is it possible for the sums of the numbers in every row and every column to be $2n$ distinct values modulo $n$, if:
a) $n = 4$;
b) $n = 5$?

In every cell of an $n \times n$ table one of the numbers $-1$, $0$, and $1$ is written. Is it possible for the sums of the numbers in every row and every column to be $2n$ distinct values, if:
a) $n = 4$;
b) $n = 5$, and all row sums are positive?

positive_text:
Let the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic sequence with initial value $a$ and common difference $2$ sum to the same total as the first $n$ terms of a geometric sequence with initial value $n$ and common ratio $2$.

a) Prove that $a+n=2^{a}$;

b) Find $a$ and $n$, if the third term of the geometric sequence equals the $23$-rd term of the arithmetic sequence.

negatives.text:
The sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic progression with first term $m$ and difference $-2$ is equal to the sum of the first $n$ terms of a geometric progression with first term $n$ and ratio $2$.

a) Prove that $m+n=2^{m}$;

b) Find $m$ and $n$, if the third term of the geometric progression is equal to the $23$-rd term of the arithmetic progression.

The sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic progression with first term $m$ and difference $2$ is equal to the sum of the first $n$ terms of a geometric progression with first term $m$ and ratio $2$.

a) Prove that $m+n=2^{m}$;

b) Find $m$ and $n$, if the third term of the geometric progression is equal to the $23$-rd term of the arithmetic progression.

The sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic progression with first term $m$ and difference $2$ is equal to the sum of the first $n$ terms of a geometric progression with first term $n$ and ratio $3$.

a) Prove that $m+n=2^{m}$;

b) Find $m$ and $n$, if the third term of the geometric progression is equal to the $23$-rd term of the arithmetic progression.

positive_text:
Let $X$ and $Y$ lie on segment $UV$ of triangle $UVW$ such that $X$ is between $U$ and $Y$. The line through $X$ parallel to $VW$ intersects the circumcircle of $\triangle XYW$ at point $R$, and the line through $X$ parallel to $YW$ intersects the circumcircle of $\triangle UXW$ at point $S$. Similarly, the line through $Y$ parallel to $VW$ intersects the circumcircle of $\triangle XYW$ at point $T$, and the line through $Y$ parallel to $XW$ intersects the circumcircle of $\triangle VYW$ at point $Z$. Show that:

a) the points $R$, $S$ and $W$ are collinear;

b) the points $R$, $S$, $T$ and $Z$ lie on a circle if and only if $UX = VY$.

negatives.text:
Let $M$ and $N$ lie on side $AB$ of triangle $ABC$, with $M$ between $A$ and $N$. The line through $M$ parallel to $AB$ intersects the circumcircle of $\triangle MNC$ at point $P$, and the line through $M$ parallel to $NC$ intersects the circumcircle of $\triangle AMC$ at point $Q$. Similarly, the line through $N$ parallel to $BC$ intersects the circumcircle of $\triangle MNC$ at point $K$, and the line through $N$ parallel to $MC$ intersects the circumcircle of $\triangle BNC$ at point $L$. Prove that:

a) the points $P$, $Q$ and $C$ are collinear;

b) the points $P$, $Q$, $K$ and $L$ are concyclic if and only if $AM = BN$.

Let $M$ and $N$ be on segment $AB$ of triangle $ABC$ such that $M$ is between $A$ and $N$. The line through $M$ parallel to $AC$ intersects the circumcircle of $\triangle MNC$ at point $P$, and the line through $M$ parallel to $NC$ intersects the circumcircle of $\triangle AMC$ at point $Q$. The line through $N$ parallel to $BC$ intersects the circumcircle of $\triangle MNC$ at point $K$, and the line through $N$ parallel to $MC$ intersects the circumcircle of $\triangle BNC$ at point $L$. Prove that:

a) the points $P$, $Q$ and $A$ are collinear;

b) the points $P$, $Q$, $K$ and $L$ are concyclic if and only if $AM = BN$.

Let $M$ and $N$ be on segment $AB$ of triangle $ABC$ such that $M$ lies between $A$ and $N$. The line through $M$ parallel to $AC$ intersects the circumcircle of $\triangle MNC$ at point $P$, and the line through $M$ parallel to $NC$ intersects the circumcircle of $\triangle AMC$ at point $Q$. The line through $N$ parallel to $BC$ intersects the circumcircle of $\triangle MNC$ at point $K$, and the line through $N$ parallel to $MC$ intersects the circumcircle of $\triangle BNC$ at point $L$. Prove that:

a) the points $P$, $Q$ and $C$ are collinear;

b) the points $P$, $Q$, $K$ and $L$ are concyclic if and only if $AM = BN + 1$.

positive_text:
In a parallelogram $PQRS$, the points $U$ and $V$ lie on $PQ$ and $QR$, respectively, such that $RU$ bisects $\angle PRV$ and $PU + QV = RV$. A line through $S$ perpendicular to $RU$ intersects side $PS$ at $T$ and diagonal $QS$ at $K$. Let $M = RU \cap PR$. Prove that:
a) $PU = RT$;
b) $QR = RS$ if $KM \parallel PS$;
c) $PQRS$ is a square if $KM \parallel PS$.

negatives.text:
Let $ABCD$ be a parallelogram. Points $E$ and $F$ lie on $AB$ and $BC$, respectively, such that $DE$ is the bisector of $\angle CDF$ and $AE + CF = DF$. The line through $C$ and perpendicular to $DE$ meets side $AD$ at $L$ and the diagonal $BD$ at $H$. Set $N = DE \cap AC$. Prove that:
a) $AE = DL$;
b) $BC = CD$ if $HN \parallel AD$;
c) $ABCD$ is a square if $HN \parallel AD$.

Let $ABCD$ be a parallelogram. Points $E$ and $F$ lie on $AB$ and $BC$, respectively, such that $DE$ is the bisector of $\angle ADF$ and $AE - CF = DF$. The line through $C$ and perpendicular to $DE$ meets side $AD$ at $L$ and the diagonal $BD$ at $H$. Set $N = DE \cap AC$. Prove that:
a) $AE = DL$;
b) $BC = CD$ if $HN \parallel AD$;
c) $ABCD$ is a square if $HN \parallel AD$.

Let $ABCD$ be a parallelogram. Points $E$ and $F$ lie on $AB$ and $BC$, respectively, such that $DE$ is the bisector of $\angle ADF$ and $AE + CF = DF$. The line through $C$ and parallel to $DE$ meets side $AD$ at $L$ and the diagonal $BD$ at $H$. Set $N = DE \cap AC$. Prove that:
a) $AE = DL$;
b) $BC = CD$ if $HN \parallel AD$;
c) $ABCD$ is a square if $HN \parallel AD$.

positive_text:
\text{Consider the equations}\\
\begin{align*}
9^{x+1.5} - 2^{x+2} &= 2^{x+5} - 9^{x+1}\\
\text{and}\\
a \cdot (5^x)^2 + |a-1| \cdot 5^x &= 1
\end{align*}
\text{where } a \text{ is a real number.}\\
\text{a) Solve the equation (1).}\\
\text{b) Find the values of } a \text{ such that the equations (1) and (2) are equivalent.}

negatives.text:
\text{Consider the equations}\\
\begin{align*}
3^{2x+3} - 2^{x+2} &= 2^{x+5} + 9^{x+1}\\
\text{and}\\
a \cdot 5^{2x} + |a-1| \cdot 5^x &= 1
\end{align*}
\text{where } a \text{ is a real number.}\\
\text{a) Solve the equation (1).}\\
\text{b) Find the values of } a \text{ such that the equations (1) and (2) are equivalent.}

\text{Consider the equations}\\
\begin{align*}
3^{2x+3} - 2^{x+2} &= 2^{x+5} - 9^{x+1}\\
\text{and}\\
a \cdot 5^{2x} + |a-1| \cdot 5^{2x} &= 1
\end{align*}
\text{where } a \text{ is a real number.}\\
\text{a) Solve the equation (1).}\\
\text{b) Find the values of } a \text{ such that the equations (1) and (2) are equivalent.}

positive_text:
Consider the recursive sequence defined by $u_1 = 2$ and $u_{n+1} = 1 + b u_n$ for all $n \geq 1$, where $b$ is a real number. Determine all values of $b$ such that:

a) the sequence forms an arithmetic progression;

b) the sequence converges, and find its limit in this case.

negatives.text:
The sequence $\{y_{n}\}_{n=1}^{\infty}$ is defined by $y_{1} = 2$ and $y_{n+1} = 1 + a y_n^2$, $n \geq 1$, where $a$ is a real number. Find all values of $a$ for which the sequence is:

a) an arithmetic progression;

b) convergent and find its limit.

A sequence $\{w_{n}\}_{n=1}^{\infty}$ is defined by $w_{1} = 2$ and $w_{n+1} = 1 + a w_n$, $n \geq 1$, where $a$ is a real number. Find all values of $a$ for which the sequence is:

a) a geometric progression;

b) convergent and find its limit.

positive_text:
Let $ g $ be a function from the positive integers to the integers satisfying the following:
1) $ g(1) = 1 $;
2) $ g(2) = 2 $;
3) $ g(mn) = g(m)g(n) $ for all $ m, n $;
4) $ g(m) > g(n) $ whenever $ m > n $.
Show that $ g(n) = n $ for all $ n $.

negatives.text:
Let $ f $ be a function from the positive integers to the integers satisfying the following:
1) $ f(n) $ is defined for all $ n $;
2) $ f(2) = -2 $;
3) $ f(mn) = f(m)f(n) $ for all $ m, n $;
4) $ f(m) > f(n) $ whenever $ m > n $.
Show that $ f(n) = n $.

Let $ f $ be a function from the positive integers to the integers satisfying the following:
1) $ f(n) $ is defined for all $ n $;
2) $ f(2) = 2 $;
3) $ f(m+n) = f(m) + f(n) $ for all $ m, n $;
4) $ f(m) > f(n) $ whenever $ m > n $.
Show that $ f(n) = n $.

Let $ f $ be a function from the positive integers to the integers satisfying the following:
1) $ f(n) $ is defined for all $ n $;
2) $ f(2) = 2 $;
3) $ f(mn) = f(m) + f(n) $ for all $ m, n $;
4) $ f(m) > f(n) $ whenever $ m > n $.
Show that $ f(n) = n $.

positive_text:
In triangle $\triangle PQR$, points $S$ and $T$ lie on $PQ$ and $PR$, respectively, with $\frac{PS}{PQ} = a$, $\frac{PT}{PR} = b$, and point $U$ lies on segment $ST$ such that $\frac{SU}{ST} = c$. Show that

(1) $S_{\triangle QSU} = (1-a)b cS_{\triangle PQR}$ and $S_{\triangle RTU} = a(1-b)(1-c)S_{\triangle PQR}$;

(2) $\sqrt[3]{S_{\triangle QSU}} + \sqrt[3]{S_{\triangle RTU}} \le \sqrt[3]{S_{\triangle PQR}}$.

negatives.text:
Let $\triangle ABC$ be a triangle. Points $D$ and $E$ are on sides $AB$ and $AC$, respectively, and point $F$ is on line segment $DE$. Let $\frac{AD}{AB} = x$, $\frac{AE}{AC} = y$, $\frac{DF}{DE} = z$. Prove that

(1) $S_{\triangle BDF} = (1+x)y zS_{\triangle ABC}$ and $S_{\triangle CEF} = x(1+y)(1-z)S_{\triangle ABC}$;

(2) $\sqrt[3]{S_{\triangle BDF}} + \sqrt[3]{S_{\triangle CEF}} \le \sqrt[3]{S_{\triangle ABC}}$.

Let $\triangle ABC$ be a triangle. Points $D$ and $E$ are on sides $AB$ and $AC$, respectively, and point $F$ is on line segment $DE$. Let $\frac{AD}{AB} = x$, $\frac{AE}{AC} = y$, $\frac{DF}{DE} = z$. Prove that

(1) $S_{\triangle BDF} = (1-x)y zS_{\triangle ABC}$ and $S_{\triangle CEF} = x(1-y)zS_{\triangle ABC}$;

(2) $\sqrt[3]{S_{\triangle BDF}} + \sqrt[3]{S_{\triangle CEF}} \le \sqrt[3]{S_{\triangle ABC}}$.

Let $\triangle ABC$ be a triangle. Points $D$ and $E$ are on sides $AB$ and $AC$, respectively, and point $F$ is on line segment $DE$. Let $\frac{AD}{AB} = x$, $\frac{AE}{AC} = y$, $\frac{DF}{DE} = z$. Prove that

(1) $S_{\triangle BDF} = (1-x)y zS_{\triangle ABC}$ and $S_{\triangle CEF} = x(1-y)(1-z)S_{\triangle ABC}$;

(2) $\sqrt[3]{S_{\triangle BDF}} + \sqrt[3]{S_{\triangle CEF}} = \sqrt[3]{S_{\triangle ABC}}$.

positive_text:
In an acute triangle $\triangle ABC$, with $\angle A < \angle B$, points $M$, $N$ are the arc midpoints of $\widehat{BC}$ and $\widehat{AC}$ (not containing $A$ and $B$ respectively) on the circumcircle $\Gamma$. A line through $C$, parallel to $MN$, intersects $\Gamma$ at $P$. The incenter of $\triangle ABC$ is $I$, and the extension of $PI$ intersects $\Gamma$ again at $T$.

(1) Prove that $MP \cdot MT = NP \cdot NT$;

(2) Let $Q$ be a point on arc $\widehat{AB}$ (not containing $C$) such that $Q \neq A, T, B$. Let $I_1$, $I_2$ be the incenters of $\triangle AQC$, $\triangle QCB$, respectively. Prove that $Q, I_1, I_2, T$ lie on a circle.

Find all non-decreasing sequences $\left(a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{2020}\right)$ of non-negative real numbers such that:
(i) $a_{2020} \leq a_{1} + 1$;
(ii) there exists a rearrangement $\left(b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{2020}\right)$ of $\left(a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{2020}\right)$ satisfying
$$
\sum_{i=1}^{2020}\left(\left(a_{i}+1\right)\left(b_{i}+1\right)\right)^{2} = 8 \sum_{i=1}^{2020} a_{i}^{3}
$$

Soit $XYZ$ un triangle. Soient $M \in [XZ]$ et $N \in [XY]$ tels que $YN = XM$. Soit $S$ le point d'intersection de $(XM)$ et $(YN)$. Les cercles circonscrits à $XNS$ et $ZMS$ se recoupent en $T$. Soient $U$ et $V$ les milieux respectifs de $[XN]$ et $[ZM]$. Soit $W$ l'intersection entre la perpendiculaire à $(TU)$ passant par $U$ et la perpendiculaire à $(TV)$ passant par $V$. Montrer que

1) $W$ se trouve sur le cercle circonscrit à $XYZ$;
2) $T$ se trouve sur la bissectrice de l'angle $\widehat{XZY}$.

negatives.text:
Soit $ABC$ un triangle. Soient $D \in [AC]$ et $E \in [AB]$ tels que $BE = 2CD$. Soit $P$ le point d'intersection de $(BD)$ et $(CE)$. Les cercles circonscrits à $BEP$ et $CDP$ se recoupent en $Q$. Soient $K$ et $L$ les milieux respectifs de $[BE]$ et $[CD]$. Soit $R$ l'intersection entre la perpendiculaire à $(QK)$ passant par $K$ et la perpendiculaire à $(QL)$ passant par $L$. Montrer que

1) $R$ se trouve sur le cercle circonscrit à $ABC$;
2) $Q$ se trouve sur la bissectrice de l'angle $\widehat{BAC}$.

Soit $ABC$ un triangle. Soient $D \in [AC]$ et $E \in [AB]$ tels que $BE = CD$. Soit $P$ le point d'intersection de $(BD)$ et $(CE)$. Les cercles circonscrits à $BEP$ et $CDP$ se recoupent en $Q$. Soient $K$ et $L$ les milieux respectifs de $[BE]$ et $[CD]$. Soit $R$ l'intersection entre la parallèle à $(QK)$ passant par $K$ et la perpendiculaire à $(QL)$ passant par $L$. Montrer que

1) $R$ se trouve sur le cercle circonscrit à $ABC$;
2) $Q$ se trouve sur la bissectrice de l'angle $\widehat{BAC}$.

positive_text:
Déterminer toutes les valeurs possibles pour $f(2018)$ où $f$ est une fonction de $\mathbb{Q}$ dans $\mathbb{R}_{+}$ vérifiant:
- $f(2) = \dfrac{1}{2}$;
- pour tout rationnel $x$, si $f(x) \leqslant 1$, alors $f(x + 1) \leqslant 1$;
- $f(x \cdot y) = f(x) \cdot f(y)$ pour tous rationnels $x$ et $y$.

negatives.text:
Trouver toutes les valeurs possibles pour $f(2018)$ où $f$ est une fonction de $\mathbb{Q}$ dans $\mathbb{R}_{+}$ vérifiant:
- $f(2) = 2$;
- pour tout rationnel $x$, si $f(x) \leqslant 1$, alors $f(x + 1) \leqslant 1$;
- $f(x + y) = f(x) + f(y)$ pour tous rationnels $x$ et $y$.

Trouver toutes les valeurs possibles pour $f(2018)$ où $f$ est une fonction de $\mathbb{Q}$ dans $\mathbb{R}_{+}$ vérifiant:
- $f(2) = \dfrac{1}{2}$;
- pour tout rationnel $x$, si $f(x) < 1$, alors $f(x + 1) < 1$;
- $f(xy) = f(x)f(y)$ pour tous rationnels $x$ et $y$.

Trouver toutes les valeurs possibles pour $f(2018)$ où $f$ est une fonction de $\mathbb{Q}$ dans $\mathbb{R}_{+}$ vérifiant:
- $f(2) = \dfrac{1}{2}$;
- pour tout rationnel $x$, si $f(x) \leqslant 1$, alors $f(x^2) \leqslant 1$;
- $f(xy) = f(x)f(y)$ pour tous rationnels $x$ et $y$.

positive_text:
Determine all integers $ n \ge 1 $ for which there exist $ n $ real numbers $ a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n $ in the interval $[-4, 2]$ satisfying the following:
- $ a_1 + a_2 + \dots + a_n \ge n $;
- $ a_1^2 + a_2^2 + \dots + a_n^2 \le 4n $;
- $ a_1^4 + a_2^4 + \dots + a_n^4 \ge 34n $.

negatives.text:
Determine all integers $ n \ge 1 $ for which there exist $ n $ real numbers $ x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n $ in $[-4, 2]$ such that:
- $ x_1 + x_2 + \dots + x_n \le n $;
- $ x_1^2 + x_2^2 + \dots + x_n^2 \le 4n $;
- $ x_1^4 + x_2^4 + \dots + x_n^4 \ge 34n $.

Determine all integers $ n \ge 1 $ for which there exist $ n $ real numbers $ x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n $ in $[-4, 2]$ such that:
- $ x_1 + x_2 + \dots + x_n \ge n $;
- $ x_1^2 + x_2^2 + \dots + x_n^2 \le 4n $;
- $ x_1^4 + x_2^4 + \dots + x_n^4 \le 34n $.

Determine all integers $ n \ge 1 $ for which there exist $ n $ real numbers $ x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n $ in $[-4, 2]$ such that:
- $ x_1 + x_2 + \dots + x_n \ge n $;
- $ x_1^2 + x_2^2 + \dots + x_n^2 \le 4n $;
- $ x_1^3 + x_2^3 + \dots + x_n^3 \ge 34n $.

positive_text:
For each $k \in \mathbb{N}$, define $\tau(k)$ as the number of positive divisors of $k$. Determine all functions $g: \mathbb{N} \rightarrow \mathbb{N}$ satisfying:
(1) $\tau(g(m)) = m$ for all $m \in \mathbb{N}$;
(2) $g(m n)$ divides $(m - 1) n^{m n - 1} g(m)$ for all $m, n \in \mathbb{N}$.

negatives.text:
For every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ let $d(n)$ be the number of positive divisors of $n$. Find all functions $f: \mathbb{N} \rightarrow \mathbb{N}$ such that:
(i) $d(f(x)) = x$ for all $x \in \mathbb{N}$;
(ii) $f(x y)$ divides $(x + 1) y^{x y - 1} f(x)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{N}$.

Let $d(n)$ be the number of positive divisors of $n$. Determine all functions $f: \mathbb{N} \rightarrow \mathbb{N}$ with the following properties:
(i) $d(f(x)) = x$ for all $x \in \mathbb{N}$;
(ii) $f(x y)$ divides $(x - 1) y^{x y} f(x)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{N}$.

positive_text:
Let triangle $ XYZ $ have side lengths $ x, y, z $ opposite to vertices $ X, Y, Z $, respectively, and let $ R $ be its circumradius. Let $ r_x, r_y, r_z $ be the exradii opposite to $ X, Y, Z $. If $ 2R \leq r_x $, show that
(i) $ x > y $ and $ x > z $;
(ii) $ 2R > r_y $ and $ 2R > r_z $.

Let $a_{1}$ be a positive integer. Define a sequence $\langle a_{n}\rangle_{n=1}^{\infty}$ such that $a_{n}$ for $n \geq 2$ is obtained by adding a nonzero digit of $a_{n-1}$ to $a_{n-1}$. Prove that
(a) the sequence contains an even number;
(b) the sequence contains infinitely many even numbers.

negatives.text:
Let $x_{1}$ be a positive integer. Define a sequence $\langle x_{n}\rangle_{n=1}^{\infty}$ such that $x_{n}$ for $n \geq 2$ is obtained by multiplying $x_{n-1}$ by one of its nonzero digits. Prove that
(a) the sequence contains an even number;
(b) the sequence contains infinitely many even numbers.

Let $x_{1}$ be a positive integer. Define a sequence $\langle x_{n}\rangle_{n=1}^{\infty}$ such that $x_{n}$ for $n \geq 2$ is obtained by adding the sum of the digits of $x_{n-1}$. Prove that
(a) the sequence contains an even number;
(b) the sequence contains infinitely many even numbers.

Let $x_{1}$ be a positive integer. Define a sequence $\langle x_{n}\rangle_{n=1}^{\infty}$ such that $x_{n}$ for $n \geq 2$ is obtained by adding some nonzero digit of $x_{n-1}$, but $x_{n} < x_{n-1}$ for all $n$. Prove that
(a) the sequence contains an even number;
(b) the sequence contains infinitely many even numbers.

positive_text:
Let $g$ be a function from $\{(a, b): a, b$ reals, $a b \neq 0\}$ to the positive real numbers satisfying
(i) $\quad g(a b, c) = g(a, c) g(b, c)$, for all $a, b \neq 0$;
(ii) $\quad g(a, b c) = g(a, b) g(a, c)$, for all $a, b \neq 0$;
(iii) $\quad g(a, 1 - a) = 1$, for all $a \neq 0, 1$.
Prove that
(a) $\quad g(a, a) = g(a, -a) = 1$, for all $a \neq 0$;
(b) $\quad g(a, b) g(b, a) = 1$, for all $a, b \neq 0$.

negatives.text:
Let $h$ be a function from $\{(p, q): p, q$ reals, $p q \neq 0\}$ to the positive real numbers satisfying
(i) $\quad h(p q, r) = h(p, r) h(q, r)$, for all $p, q \neq 0$;
(ii) $\quad h(p, q r) = h(p, q) h(p, r)$, for all $p, q \neq 0$;
(iii) $\quad h(p, 1 + p) = 1$, for all $p \neq 0, -1$.
Prove that
(a) $\quad h(p, p) = h(p, -p) = 1$, for all $p \neq 0$;
(b) $\quad h(p, q) h(q, p) = 1$, for all $p, q \neq 0$.

Let $k$ be a function from $\{(m, n): m, n$ reals, $m n \neq 0\}$ to the positive real numbers satisfying
(i) $\quad k(m n, p) = k(m, p) k(n, p)$, for all $m, n \neq 0$;
(ii) $\quad k(m, n p) = k(m, n) k(m, p)$, for all $m, n \neq 0$;
(iii) $\quad k(m, 1/m) = 1$, for all $m \neq 0$.
Prove that
(a) $\quad k(m, m) = k(m, -m) = 1$, for all $m \neq 0$;
(b) $\quad k(m, n) k(n, m) = 1$, for all $m, n \neq 0$.

Let $l$ be a function from $\{(u, v): u, v$ reals, $u v \neq 0\}$ to the positive real numbers satisfying
(i) $\quad l(u v, w) = l(u, w) l(v, w)$, for all $u, v \neq 0$;
(ii) $\quad l(u, v w) = l(u, v) l(u, w)$, for all $u, v \neq 0$;
(iii) $\quad l(u, 1 - u) = u$, for all $u \neq 0, 1$.
Prove that
(a) $\quad l(u, u) = l(u, -u) = 1$, for all $u \neq 0$;
(b) $\quad l(u, v) l(v, u) = 1$, for all $u, v \neq 0$.

positive_text:
Two circles $A$ and $B$, with centres at $P$ and $Q$ respectively, touch at point $T$. The circle with diameter $PQ$ intersects circle $A$ at $R$ and circle $B$ at $S$, with both $R$ and $S$ on the same side of the line $PQ$. When segment $RS$ is extended, it intersects circle $A$ again at $X$ and circle $B$ again at $Y$. Prove that
(a) $|RX| = |SY|$;
(b) the line through $T$ perpendicular to $PQ$ bisects segment $RS$.

negatives.text:
Two circles $C_1$ and $C_2$, with centres at $D$ and $E$ respectively, touch at $B$. The circle with diameter $DE$ intersects the circle $C_1$ at $H$ and the circle $C_2$ at $K$, with $H$ and $K$ on the same side of the line $DE$. When segment $HK$ is extended, it intersects the **line** $C_1$ at $L$ and the **line** $C_2$ at $M$. Prove that
(a) $|LH| = |KM|$;
(b) the line through $B$ perpendicular to $DE$ bisects $HK$.

Two circles $C_1$ and $C_2$, with centres at $D$ and $E$ respectively, touch at $B$. The circle with diameter $DE$ intersects the circle $C_1$ at $H$ and the circle $C_2$ at $K$, with $H$ and $K$ on the **opposite sides** of the line $DE$. When segment $HK$ is extended, it intersects circle $C_1$ again at $L$ and circle $C_2$ again at $M$. Prove that
(a) $|LH| = |KM|$;
(b) the line through $B$ perpendicular to $DE$ bisects $HK$.

Two circles $C_1$ and $C_2$, with centres at $D$ and $E$ respectively, intersect at points $B$ and $C$. The circle with diameter $DE$ intersects the circle $C_1$ at $H$ and the circle $C_2$ at $K$, with $H$ and $K$ on the same side of the line $DE$. When segment $HK$ is extended, it intersects circle $C_1$ again at $L$ and circle $C_2$ again at $M$. Prove that
(a) $|LH| = |KM|$;
(b) the line through $B$ perpendicular to $DE$ bisects $HK$.

positive_text:
Sia $a_{0}, a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots$ una successione di numeri razionali definita in questo modo: $a_{0}$ è un numero razionale arbitrario, e per ogni $n \geq 0$,
$$
a_{n+1} = \begin{cases}
\left|\frac{a_{n}}{2}-1\right| & \text{se il numeratore di } a_{n} \text{ è divisibile per 2}, \\
\left|\frac{1}{a_{n}}-1\right| & \text{se il numeratore di } a_{n} \text{ è congruo a 1 modulo 2},
\end{cases}
$$
dove il numeratore è inteso ridotto ai minimi termini. Provare che per ogni valore iniziale $a_{0}$:
(a) la successione assume un numero finito di valori distinti;
(b) esiste esattamente un elemento nella successione che è 0 oppure $2/3$.

Sia $n>1$ un intero fissato. Luca e Giada giocano il seguente gioco:

1. Luca scrive un numero intero positivo;
2. Giada, a sua volta, sceglie un numero intero maggiore di 1 che sia divisore o multiplo del numero di Luca (incluso lo stesso);
3. Luca modifica il numero scelto da Giada aggiungendo o sottraendo 1;
il gioco prosegue con mosse alternate. Giada vince se riesce a far comparire $n$ entro 50 turni. Per quali $n$ Giada può vincere indipendentemente da come Luca gioca?

negatives.text:
Dato $n > 1$, due giocatori, Luca e Sofia, giocano il seguente gioco:

1. Luca inizia con un numero intero positivo;
2. Sofia sceglie un numero intero maggiore di 1 che è un divisore o un multiplo del quadrato del numero di Luca;
3. Luca modifica il numero di Sofia aggiungendo o togliendo 1;
il gioco si ripete. Sofia vince se riesce a far apparire $n$ entro 50 turni. Per quali $n$ Sofia può vincere, indipendentemente da come gioca Luca?

Dato $n > 1$, due giocatori, Matteo e Alice, giocano il seguente gioco:

1. Matteo inizia con un numero intero positivo;
2. Alice sceglie un numero intero maggiore di 1 che è un divisore o un multiplo di quello di Matteo;
3. Matteo modifica il numero di Alice aggiungendo o togliendo 2;
il gioco si ripete. Alice vince se riesce a far apparire $n$ entro 50 turni. Per quali $n$ Alice può vincere, indipendentemente da come gioca Matteo?

positive_text:
Sia $m \geq 1$ un numero naturale. Determinare in funzione di $m$ il numero di interi positivi $a$ con le seguenti proprietà:

1. in base dieci hanno esattamente $m$ cifre, ciascuna dispari;

2. sono divisibili per $5$, e il numero $\frac{a}{5}$, espresso in base dieci, ha anch'esso esattamente $m$ cifre, ciascuna dispari.

negatives.text:
Sia $k \geq 1$ un numero naturale. Determinare in funzione di $k$ il numero di interi positivi $n$ con le seguenti proprietà:

a. in base dieci si scrivono con $k$ cifre, tutte dispari;

b. sono divisibili per $2$, e il quoziente $\frac{n}{2}$, scritto in base dieci, ha ancora $k$ cifre, tutte dispari.

Sia $k \geq 1$ un numero naturale. Determinare in funzione di $k$ il numero di interi positivi $n$ con le seguenti proprietà:

a. in base dieci si scrivono con $k$ cifre, tutte dispari;

b. sono divisibili per $5$, e il quoziente $\frac{n}{5}$, scritto in base dieci, ha $k-1$ cifre, tutte dispari.

Sia $k \geq 1$ un numero naturale. Determinare in funzione di $k$ il numero di interi positivi $n$ con le seguenti proprietà:

a. in base dieci si scrivono con $k$ cifre, di cui almeno una dispari;

b. sono divisibili per $5$, e il quoziente $\frac{n}{5}$, scritto in base dieci, ha $k$ cifre, tutte dispari.

positive_text:
Trovare tutte le funzioni $g$, definite sull'insieme $\mathbb{Z}$ dei numeri interi e a valori in $\mathbb{R}$, tali che:
- $g(a) < g(b)$ per ogni $a, b \in \mathbb{Z}$ con $a < b$;
- per ogni $a, b \in \mathbb{Z}$, esiste un $c \in \mathbb{Z}$ tale che $g(a) - g(b) = g(c)$.

negatives.text:
Trovare tutte le funzioni $h$, definite su $\mathbb{Z}$ e a valori in $\mathbb{R}$, tali che:
- $h(a) < h(b)$ per ogni $a < b$;
- per ogni $a, b \in \mathbb{Z}$, esiste un $c \in \mathbb{Z}$ tale che $h(a) + h(b) = h(c)$.

Trovare tutte le funzioni $p$, definite su $\mathbb{Z}$ e a valori in $\mathbb{R}$, tali che:
- $p(a) < p(b)$ per ogni $a < b$;
- per ogni $a, b \in \mathbb{Z}$, esiste un $c \in \mathbb{Z}$ tale che $p(a) - p(b) = 2p(c)$.

Trovare tutte le funzioni $q$, definite su $\mathbb{Z}$ e a valori in $\mathbb{R}$, tali che:
- $q(a) < q(b)$ per ogni $a < b$;
- per ogni $a, b \in \mathbb{Z}$, esiste un $c \in \mathbb{Z}$ tale che $q(a) - q(b) = q(c+1)$.

positive_text:
In un triangolo acutangolo $DEF$ con $DE < DF$, la bisettrice da $D$ interseca $EF$ nel punto $P$. Tracciando una retta parallela a $DE$ passante per $P$, essa incontra $DF$ nel punto $Q$. Su questa retta, consideriamo il punto $R$ che si trova sulla semiretta opposta a $Q$ rispetto a $P$ e tale che $QR = QD$. Sia $S$ la proiezione di $R$ su $EF$ e $T$ l'intersezione di $DF$ con la retta passante per $P$ e perpendicolare a $DP$.

a. Dimostrare che il circocentro di $DPR$ è $Q$;

b. Dimostrare che $\triangle STF$ è simile a $\triangle DPF$.

negatives.text:
In un triangolo acutangolo $ABC$ con $AB < AC$, la bisettrice da $A$ interseca $BC$ in $P$. La parallela a $AB$ passante per $P$ incontra $AC$ in $Q$. Su questa retta, prendiamo un punto $R$ tale che $QR = 2QA$ e che si trovi sulla semiretta opposta a $Q$ rispetto a $P$. Sia $S$ la proiezione di $R$ su $BC$ e $T$ l'intersezione di $AC$ con la retta passante per $P$ e perpendicolare a $AP$.

a. Dimostrare che il circocentro di $APR$ è $Q$;

b. Dimostrare che $\triangle STC$ è simile a $\triangle APC$.

