positive_text:
A unique number is a positive integer $ m $ for which there exist positive integers $ x $, $ y $, $ z $, and $ w $ such that $$ m = \frac{x^3 + 2y^3}{z^3 + 2w^3} $$ Prove that
(a) There are infinitely many unique numbers;
(b) 2014 is not a unique number.

negatives.text:
A unique number is a positive integer $ m $ for which there exist positive integers $ x $, $ y $, $ z $, and $ w $ such that $$ m = \frac{x^3 + 2y^3}{z^3 + 2w^2} $$ Prove that
(a) There are infinitely many unique numbers;
(b) 2014 is not a unique number.

A unique number is a positive integer $ m $ for which there exist positive integers $ x $, $ y $, $ z $, and $ w $ such that $$ m = \frac{x^3 + 2y^3}{z^3 - 2w^3} $$ Prove that
(a) There are infinitely many unique numbers;
(b) 2014 is not a unique number.

A unique number is a positive integer $ m $ for which there exist positive integers $ x $, $ y $, $ z $, and $ w $ such that $$ m = \frac{x^3 + 2y^3}{z^3 + 2w^3 + 1} $$ Prove that
(a) There are infinitely many unique numbers;
(b) 2014 is not a unique number.

positive_text:
Let $S$ be a finite non-empty collection of distinct prime numbers. Define $|S|$ as the number of elements in $S$, and $k(S)$ as the maximum number of consecutive positive integers such that each is divisible by at least one prime in $S$.
(i) Prove that $|S| \le k(S)$, with equality if and only if the smallest element in $S$ is strictly greater than $|S|$;
(ii) Prove that $k(S) < (|S| + 1)(2^{|S|} - 1)$.

negatives.text:
Let $Q$ be a finite non-empty set of integers greater than 1, not necessarily prime. Define $|Q|$ as the size of $Q$, and $c(Q)$ as the maximum number of consecutive positive integers such that each is divisible by at least one element of $Q$.
(i) Prove that $|Q| \le c(Q)$, with equality if and only if the smallest element of $Q$ is greater than $|Q|$;
(ii) Prove that $c(Q) < (|Q| + 1)(2^{|Q|} - 1)$.

Let $R$ be a finite non-empty set of primes. Define $|R|$ as the number of primes in $R$, and $d(R)$ as the maximum number of consecutive positive integers such that each is divisible by a distinct prime in $R$.
(i) Prove that $|R| \le d(R)$, with equality if and only if the smallest element in $R$ is greater than $|R|$;
(ii) Prove that $d(R) < (|R| + 1)(2^{|R|} - 1)$.

Let $T$ be a finite non-empty set of primes. Define $|T|$ as the number of elements in $T$, and $e(T)$ as the maximum number of consecutive positive integers such that each is divisible by an element of $T$ or is a power of an element of $T$.
(i) Prove that $|T| \le e(T)$, with equality if and only if the smallest element in $T$ is greater than $|T|$;
(ii) Prove that $e(T) < (|T| + 1)(2^{|T|} - 1)$.

positive_text:
Let $P(x, y)$ be a polynomial with integer coefficients. Suppose there exist two points $M = (m_1, m_2)$ and $N = (n_1, n_2)$ such that:
(a) $M$ is an integer lattice point;
(b) the Manhattan distance between $M$ and $N$ is 2010;
(c) $P(k, l) > P(m_1, m_2)$ for all integer pairs $(k, l) 
eq (m_1, m_2)$;
(d) $P(x, y) > P(n_1, n_2)$ for all real points $(x, y) 
eq (n_1, n_2)$. Determine whether such a polynomial and points exist.

negatives.text:
Suppose there exists a polynomial $f(x, y)$ with integer coefficients and two points $A = (a_1, a_2)$ and $B = (b_1, b_2)$ such that:
(i) $A$ is an integer point;
(ii) $|a_1 - b_1| + |a_2 - b_2| = 2010$;
(iii) $f(n_1, n_2) < f(a_1, a_2)$ for all integer points $(n_1, n_2) 
eq A$;
(iv) $f(x_1, x_2) > f(b_1, b_2)$ for all points $(x_1, x_2) 
eq B$. Determine whether such a polynomial and points exist.

Suppose $f(x, y)$ is a polynomial with rational coefficients and $A = (a_1, a_2)$, $B = (b_1, b_2)$ are two points such that:
(i) $A$ is an integer point;
(ii) $|a_1 - b_1| + |a_2 - b_2| = 2010$;
(iii) $f(n_1, n_2) > f(a_1, a_2)$ for all integer points $(n_1, n_2) 
eq A$;
(iv) $f(x_1, x_2) > f(b_1, b_2)$ for all real points $(x_1, x_2) 
eq B$. Determine whether such a polynomial and points exist.

positive_text:
Let $ABCD$ be an isosceles trapezoid with $AB \parallel CD$ and $AC \perp BD$. A line through the diagonals' intersection point is drawn parallel to the bases and intersects $BC$ at $P$ and $AD$ at $R$. If $Q$ is the reflection of $P$ over the midpoint of $BC$, prove that

a) $QR = AD$;

b) $QR \perp AD$.

negatives.text:
Let $ABCD$ be an isosceles trapezoid with $AB \parallel CD$ and diagonals $AC$ and $BD$ intersecting at a point. A line through the intersection point, parallel to the bases, meets $BC$ at $P$ and $AD$ at $R$. If $Q$ is the reflection of $P$ over the midpoint of $AB$, prove that

a) $QR = AD$;

b) $QR \perp AD$.

Let $ABCD$ be an isosceles trapezoid with $AB \parallel CD$ and diagonals $AC$ and $BD$ intersecting at a point. A line through the intersection point, parallel to the bases, meets $BC$ at $P$ and $AD$ at $R$. If $Q$ is the reflection of $P$ over the midpoint of $BC$, prove that

a) $QR = CD$;

b) $QR \perp AD$.

Let $ABCD$ be an isosceles trapezoid with $AB \parallel CD$ and diagonals $AC$ and $BD$ intersecting at a point. A line through the intersection point, parallel to the bases, meets $BC$ at $P$ and $AD$ at $R$. If $Q$ is the reflection of $P$ over the midpoint of $BC$, prove that

a) $QR = AD$;

b) $QR \parallel AD$.

positive_text:
Determine all functions $ g: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N} $ such that:
(i) $ g(a + b) = g(a) + g(b) + 2ab $, for all integers $ a, b $;
(ii) $ g(g(x)) - g(1) $ is a square number for all integers $ x $.

negatives.text:
Find all functions $ h: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N} $ such that:
(i) $ h(m+n) = h(m) + h(n) - 2mn $, for all integers $ m, n $;
(ii) $ h(h(x)) - h(1) $ is a perfect square for all integers $ x $.

Determine all functions $ k: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N} $ such that:
(i) $ k(m+n) = k(m) + k(n) + 2mn $, for all integers $ m, n $;
(ii) $ k(k(x)) + k(1) $ is a perfect square for all integers $ x $.

Find all functions $ p: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N} $ such that:
(i) $ p(m+n) = p(m) + p(n) + 2mn $, for all integers $ m, n $;
(ii) $ p(p(x)) - p(0) $ is a perfect square for all integers $ x $.

positive_text:
Let $J \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $g : J \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $g(a) - g(b) < |a - b|$, for every $a, b \in J$;

ii) $\int_a^b g(t)\,dt - g(a)(b-a) \le \frac{1}{2}(a-b)^2$, for every $a, b \in J$.

negatives.text:
Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $f(x) - f(y) < |x - y|^2$, for every $x, y \in I$;

ii) $\int_x^y f(t)\,dt - f(x)(y - x) \le \frac{1}{2}(x - y)^2$, for every $x, y \in I$.

Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $f(x) - f(y) < |x - y|$, for every $x, y \in I$;

ii) $\int_x^y f(t)\,dt - f(x)(y - x) \le (x - y)^2$, for every $x, y \in I$.

positive_text:
Let $f: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}^*$ be a strictly increasing function. Prove that:
a) there exists a decreasing sequence of positive real numbers, $(z_k)_{k \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $z_k \le 2z_{f(k)}$, for all $k \in \mathbb{N}$;
b) if $(a_k)_{k \in \mathbb{N}}$ is a decreasing sequence of real numbers, converging to $0$, then there exists a decreasing sequence of real numbers $(z_k)_{k \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $a_k \le z_k \le 2z_{f(k)}$, for all $k \in \mathbb{N}$.

negatives.text:
Let $f: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}^*$ be a strictly increasing function. Prove that:
a) there exists an increasing sequence of positive real numbers, $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$;
b) if $(x_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ is a decreasing sequence of real numbers, converging to $0$, then there exists a decreasing sequence of real numbers $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $x_n \le y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$.

Let $f: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}^*$ be a strictly increasing function. Prove that:
a) there exists a decreasing sequence of positive real numbers, $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$;
b) if $(x_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ is an increasing sequence of real numbers, converging to $0$, then there exists a decreasing sequence of real numbers $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $x_n \le y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$.

positive_text:
Determine all differentiable functions $ g : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} $ satisfying the following properties: 
(i) $ g'(n) = 0 $ for every integer $ n $; 
(ii) whenever $ g'(x) = 0 $ for some $ x \in \mathbb{R} $, it must be that $ g(x) = 0 $.

In a right triangle $PQR$ with $\angle Q = 90^\circ$, let $I$ be the incenter and $S$, $T$, and $U$ be the points of tangency of the incircle with sides $[PQ]$, $[QR]$, and $[PR]$, respectively. If $RI \cap SU = \{V\}$ and $TV \cap PQ = \{W\}$, prove that:
a) $PI = TW$;
b) $SV = \frac{UI \cdot UV}{UR}$.

negatives.text:
In a triangle $ABC$ with $\angle B = 90^\circ$, the incircle touches sides $[AB]$, $[BC]$, and $[AC]$ at points $F$, $D$, and $E$, respectively. If $CI \cap EF = \{M\}$ and $DM \cap AC = \{N\}$, prove that:
a) $AI = ND$;
b) $FM = \frac{EI \cdot EM}{EC}$.

positive_text:
Să se determine funcţiile derivabile $g: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ care satisfac simultan condiţiile:
i) $g'(x) = 0$, pentru orice $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;
ii) dacă $g'(x) = 0$ pentru un $x \in \mathbb{R}$, atunci $g(x) = 0$.

negatives.text:
Să se determine funcțiile derivabile $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ care verifică:
i) $f'(x) = 0$ pentru orice $x \in \mathbb{Q}$;
ii) dacă $f'(x) = 0$, atunci $f(x) = 0$.

Găsiți funcțiile derivabile $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ care satisfac:
i) $f'(x) = 0$ pentru orice $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;
ii) dacă $f(x) = 0$, atunci $f'(x) = 0$.

Să se determine funcțiile derivabile $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ pentru care:
i) $f'(x) = 0$ pentru orice $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;
ii) dacă $f'(x) = 0$, atunci $f(x) = 1$.

positive_text:
Let $X$ and $Y$ be positive integers, and define $\square(XY)$ to be the number formed by concatenating the digits of $Y$ to the right of the digits of $X$. For example, if $X = 712$ and $Y = 49$, then $\square(XY) = 71249$. Show that for each of the following cases, there are infinitely many perfect squares of the form $\square(XY)$:

i) $X$ and $Y$ are perfect squares;

ii) $X$ and $Y$ are perfect cubes;

iii) $X$ is a perfect cube and $Y$ is a perfect square;

iv) $X$ is a perfect square and $Y$ is a perfect cube.

negatives.text:
Define $\overline{AB}$ as before. Prove that there are infinitely many perfect cubes of the form $\overline{AB}$ in each of the following situations:

a) $A$ and $B$ are perfect squares;

b) $A$ and $B$ are perfect cubes;

c) $A$ is a perfect cube and $B$ is a perfect square;

d) $A$ is a perfect square and $B$ is a perfect cube.

Define $\overline{AB}$ as before. Prove that there are infinitely many numbers of the form $\overline{AB}$ that are divisible by 100 in each of the following situations:

a) $A$ and $B$ are perfect squares;

b) $A$ and $B$ are perfect cubes;

c) $A$ is a perfect cube and $B$ is a perfect square;

d) $A$ is a perfect square and $B$ is a perfect cube.

Let $\overline{AB}$ be as defined. Prove that there are infinitely many prime numbers of the form $\overline{AB}$ in each of the following situations:

a) $A$ and $B$ are perfect squares;

b) $A$ and $B$ are perfect cubes;

c) $A$ is a perfect cube and $B$ is a perfect square;

d) $A$ is a perfect square and $B$ is a perfect cube.

positive_text:
Let $h: [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ be continuous and satisfy $h((1-s)a + sb) \le \max\{h(a), h(b)\}$ for all $s, a, b \in [0, 1]$. Denote $c = \inf_{0 \le x \le 1} h(x)$. Prove:
a) the set $\{x \mid 0 \le x \le 1, h(x) = c\}$ is a closed interval;
b) if $h(0) = 0$, $\int_0^1 h(x)\, dx = 0$, and $k: [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ is non-decreasing, then $\int_0^1 h(x)k(x)\, dx \ge 0$.

Consider a sequence $(b_n)_{n \ge 1}$ of positive integers with common difference $d$ and initial term $b_1 = a$. Let $T_n = b_1^2 + b_2^2 + \dots + b_n^2$. Prove that:

a) if $p \ge 5$ is a prime, then $p$ divides $T_p$;

b) $T_5$ is not a perfect square.

negatives.text:
Let $(c_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic sequence of positive integers and define $U_n = c_1^3 + c_2^3 + \dots + c_n^3$. Prove that:

a) if $p \ge 5$ is a prime, then $p$ divides $U_p$;

b) $U_5$ is not a cube.

Let $(d_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be a geometric sequence of positive integers and define $V_n = d_1^2 + d_2^2 + \dots + d_n^2$. Prove that:

a) if $p \ge 5$ is a prime, then $p$ divides $V_p$;

b) $V_5$ is not a square.

Let $(e_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic sequence of positive integers and define $W_n = e_1 + e_2 + \dots + e_n$. Prove that:

a) if $p \ge 5$ is a prime, then $p$ divides $W_p$;

b) $W_5$ is not a square.

positive_text:
ext{Let } n \ge 2 	ext{ be an integer. Consider an } n 	imes n 	ext{ grid. The main diagonal is marked with shaded cells. All cells below the main diagonal are filled with 0, and the remaining cells (including the shaded ones) are filled with positive integers. After filling, we compute the sum of the entries in each row and column, resulting in } 2n 	ext{ sums. The grid is called *magic* if these } 2n 	ext{ sums are a permutation of } 1, 2, \dots, 2n.

ext{a) Prove that no } 5 	imes 5 	ext{ grid is magic.}

ext{b) What is the maximum possible value of any entry in a } 4 	imes 4 	ext{ magic grid?}

negatives.text:
ext{Let } n \ge 2 	ext{ be an integer. Consider an } n 	imes n 	ext{ grid. The main diagonal is marked with shaded cells. All cells below the main diagonal are filled with 0, and the remaining cells (including the shaded ones) are filled with positive integers. After filling, we compute the sum of the entries in each row and column, resulting in } 2n 	ext{ sums. The grid is called *magic* if these } 2n 	ext{ sums are a permutation of } 1, 2, \dots, 2n-1, 2n+1.

ext{a) Prove that no } 5 	imes 5 	ext{ grid is magic.}

ext{b) What is the maximum possible value of any entry in a } 4 	imes 4 	ext{ magic grid?}

positive_text:
Fie $n \in \mathbb{N}^{*}, n \geq 2$. Demonstraţi că, pentru orice numere complexe $x_{1}, x_{2}, \ldots, x_{n}$ şi $y_{1}, y_{2}, \ldots, y_{n}$, următoarele afirmaţii sunt echivalente:

a) $\sum_{j=1}^{n}\left|w-x_{j}\right|^{2} \leq \sum_{j=1}^{n}\left|w-y_{j}\right|^{2}$, pentru orice $w \in \mathbb{C}$;

b) $\sum_{j=1}^{n} x_{j} = \sum_{j=1}^{n} y_{j}$ şi $\sum_{j=1}^{n}\left|x_{j}\right|^{2} \leq \sum_{j=1}^{n}\left|y_{j}\right|^{2}$.

negatives.text:
Fie $n \in \mathbb{N}^{*}, n \geq 2$. Demonstraţi că, pentru orice numere complexe $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ şi $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$, următoarele afirmaţii sunt echivalente:

a) $\sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|z-a_{k}\right|^{2} \geq \sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|z-b_{k}\right|^{2}$, pentru orice $z \in \mathbb{C}$;

b) $\sum_{k=1}^{n} a_{k} = \sum_{k=1}^{n} b_{k}$ şi $\sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|a_{k}\right|^{2} \leq \sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|b_{k}\right|^{2}$.

Fie $n \in \mathbb{N}^{*}, n \geq 2$. Demonstraţi că, pentru orice numere complexe $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ şi $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$, următoarele afirmaţii sunt echivalente:

a) $\sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|z-a_{k}\right|^{2} \leq \sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|z-b_{k}\right|^{2}$, pentru orice $z \in \mathbb{C}$;

b) $\sum_{k=1}^{n} a_{k} = \sum_{k=1}^{n} b_{k}$ şi $\sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|a_{k}\right|^{2} < \sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|b_{k}\right|^{2}$.

Fie $n \in \mathbb{N}^{*}, n \geq 2$. Demonstraţi că, pentru orice numere complexe $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ şi $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$, următoarele afirmaţii sunt echivalente:

a) $\sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|z-a_{k}\right|^{2} \leq \sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|z-b_{k}\right|^{2}$, pentru orice $z \in \mathbb{C}$;

b) $\sum_{k=1}^{n} \operatorname{Re}(a_{k}) = \sum_{k=1}^{n} \operatorname{Re}(b_{k})$ şi $\sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|a_{k}\right|^{2} \leq \sum_{k=1}^{n}\left|b_{k}\right|^{2}$.

positive_text:
În $n$ containere sunt distribuite două tipuri de obiecte: roşii şi albastre. Trebuie selectate $\frac{n}{2}$ containere astfel încât ele să conţină cel puţin $\frac{1}{2}$ din obiectele roşii şi cel puţin $\frac{1}{2}$ din obiectele albastre. Este întotdeauna posibilă o alegere indiferent de numărul obiectelor şi de modul în care au fost distribuite dacă:

a) $n=100$;

b) $n=99$ ?

negatives.text:
În $n$ cutii transparente se introduc bile roşii şi bile albastre. Trebuie alese 50 de cutii astfel încât ele să conţină împreună cel puţin $\frac{2}{3}$ din bilele roşii sau cel puţin $\frac{2}{3}$ din bilele albastre. Este întotdeauna posibilă o alegere indiferent de numărul bilelor şi de modul în care au fost distribuite dacă:

a) $n=100$;

b) $n=99$ ?

În $n$ cutii transparente se introduc bile roşii sau bile albastre. Trebuie alese 50 de cutii astfel încât ele să conţină împreună cel puţin jumătate din bilele roşii sau cel puţin jumătate din bilele albastre. Este întotdeauna posibilă o alegere indiferent de numărul bilelor şi de modul în care au fost distribuite dacă:

a) $n=100$;

b) $n=99$ ?

În $n$ cutii transparente se introduc bile roşii şi bile albastre. Trebuie alese 50 de cutii astfel încât ele să conţină împreună cel puţin jumătate din bilele roşii sau cel puţin jumătate din bilele albastre. Este întotdeauna posibilă o alegere indiferent de numărul bilelor şi de modul în care au fost distribuite dacă:

a) $n=100$;

b) $n=99$ ?

positive_text:
Let $ADEF$ be a square and $G$ an arbitrary point on the side $(EF)$. On the outside of the triangle $AGD$ one considers the squares $GHID$ and $GJKL$. Prove that:

a) $IH = KL$;

b) $IH \perp KL$.

negatives.text:
Let $ABCD$ be a square and $E$ an arbitrary point on the side $(CD)$. On the outside of the triangle $ABE$ one considers the squares $ENMA$ and $EBQP$. Prove that:

a) $NA = PC$;

b) $NA \perp PC$.

Let $ABCD$ be a square and $E$ an arbitrary point on the side $(CD)$. On the outside of the triangle $ABE$ one considers the squares $ENMA$ and $EBQP$. Prove that:

a) $ND = QC$;

b) $ND \perp QC$.

Let $ABCD$ be a square and $E$ an arbitrary point on the side $(CD)$. On the outside of the triangle $ABE$ one considers the squares $ENMA$ and $EBQP$. Prove that:

a) $ND = PC$;

b) $ND \parallel PC$.

positive_text:
Determine all functions $g : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ such that:
(i) $g(g(a^2) + b + g(b)) = a^2 + 2g(b)$;
(ii) $a \le b$ implies $g(a) \le g(b)$;
for all real numbers $a$ and $b$.

negatives.text:
Find all functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying:
(i) $f(f(x^2) + y + f(y)) = x + 2f(y)$;
(ii) $x \le y$ implies $f(x) \le f(y)$;
for all real $x$ and $y$.

Find all functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying:
(i) $f(f(x^2) + y + f(y)) = x^2 + f(y)$;
(ii) $x \le y$ implies $f(x) \le f(y)$;
for all real $x$ and $y$.

Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be such that:
(i) $f(f(x^2) + y + f(y)) = x^2 + 2f(y)$;
(ii) $x \le y$ implies $f(x) \ge f(y)$;
for all real $x$ and $y$.
Find all such functions $f$.

positive_text:
Let $m$ be a positive integer. Find the smallest number $n \ge m + 1$ such that the following game can be continued forever:

There are $n$ jars, denoted $j_1, j_2, \dots, j_n$, each containing initially $i$ tokens in jar $j_i$. In each move, perform these steps:

(1) Pick $m+1$ jars;

(2) From these $m+1$, select $m$ and remove at least half the tokens from each, then add $i$ tokens to the remaining jar $j_i$;

(3) If any jar becomes empty, the game ends; otherwise, repeat.

negatives.text:
Let $k$ be a positive integer. Determine the least integer $n \ge k + 1$ for which the game below can be played indefinitely:

There are $n$ boxes, labelled $b_1, b_2, \dots, b_n$, each containing $i$ coins in $b_i$. In each move:

(1) Select $k+1$ boxes;

(2) From these $k+1$, remove at least half of the coins from each and distribute them equally among the $k+1$ boxes;

(3) If a box becomes empty, the game ends; otherwise, repeat.

Let $k$ be a positive integer. Determine the least integer $n \ge k + 1$ for which the game below can be played indefinitely:

There are $n$ boxes, each initially with $i$ coins in box $b_i$. In each move:

(1) Select $k+1$ boxes;

(2) From these $k+1$, choose $k$ and remove exactly half of the coins from each, and add all removed coins to the remaining box;

(3) If a box becomes empty, the game ends; otherwise, repeat.

Let $k$ be a positive integer. Determine the least integer $n \ge k + 1$ for which the game below can be played indefinitely:

There are $n$ boxes, each containing $i$ coins in box $b_i$. In each move:

(1) Select $k+1$ boxes;

(2) From these $k+1$, choose $k$ and remove at least half of the coins from each, and add the removed coins to the remaining box;

(3) If a box becomes empty, the game ends; otherwise, repeat.

positive_text:
Demonstrați că pentru orice număr întreg pozitiv $n \geqslant 2$, există un multiplu nenul $m$ al lui $n$ astfel încât:

a) $m < n^{4}$;

b) $m$ conține în reprezentarea sa zecimală cel mult patru tipuri de cifre.

negatives.text:
Arătați că pentru orice număr natural $n \geqslant 2$, există un multiplu $m$ al său, nenul, cu următoarele proprietăți:

a) $m < n^{2}$;

b) în scrierea lui $m$ în baza 10 se folosesc cel mult patru cifre distincte.

Arătați că pentru orice număr natural $n \geqslant 2$, există un multiplu $m$ al său, nenul, cu următoarele proprietăți:

a) $m < n^{4}$;

b) în scrierea lui $m$ în baza 10 se folosesc exact patru cifre distincte.

positive_text:
Fie $\beta$ un număr din intervalul $(0,1)$. Demonstrați că există un șir $\left(a_{k}\right)_{k \geq 1}$, cu $a_{k} \in \{0,1\}$, astfel încât pentru orice $n \geq 2$, inegalitatea
$$
0 \leq \beta - 2n \sum_{k=1}^{n} \frac{a_k}{(n+k-1)(n+k)}
\leq \frac{2}{n+1}
$$
este adevărată.

Let $ g : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} $ be a continuous function such that $ g(g(x)) = x^{2023} $ for all $ x \in \mathbb{R} $. Show that:

a) $ 2023\sqrt[2023]{g(x)} = g(2023\sqrt[2023]{x}) $, for all $ x \in \mathbb{R} $;

b) for every integer $ n \ge 3 $, there exists an arithmetic sequence $ a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n $ such that $ \sum_{i=1}^{n} g(a_i) = 0 $.

negatives.text:
Let $ h : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} $ be a continuous function such that $ h(h(x)) = x^{2023} + 1 $ for all $ x \in \mathbb{R} $. Show that:

a) $ 2023\sqrt[2023]{h(x)} = h(2023\sqrt[2023]{x}) $, for all $ x \in \mathbb{R} $;

b) for every $ n \ge 3 $, there exists an arithmetic progression $ y_1, y_2, \dots, y_n $ such that $ \sum_{k=1}^{n} h(y_k) = 0 $.

Let $ k : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} $ be a continuous function such that $ k(k(x)) = x^{2024} $ for all $ x \in \mathbb{R} $. Show that:

a) $ 2024\sqrt[2024]{k(x)} = k(2024\sqrt[2024]{x}) $, for all $ x \in \mathbb{R} $;

b) for every $ n \ge 3 $, there exists an arithmetic progression $ z_1, z_2, \dots, z_n $ such that $ \sum_{k=1}^{n} k(z_k) = 0 $.

Let $ m : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} $ be a continuous function such that $ m(m(x)) = x^{2023} $ for all $ x \in \mathbb{R} $. Show that:

a) $ 2023\sqrt[2023]{m(x)} = m(2023\sqrt[2023]{x}) $, for all $ x \in \mathbb{R} $;

b) for every $ n \ge 3 $, there exists a geometric progression $ w_1, w_2, \dots, w_n $ such that $ \sum_{k=1}^{n} m(w_k) = 0 $.

Let $x_0$ be a real number. Prove that there exists a useful function $f$ such that $f(x_0) = 0$ if and only if $|x_0| \ge 4$.

We define a function $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ to be *useful* if it satisfies:
(i) $|f(x)| \le 2$ for all $x$ with $|x| \le 2$;
(ii) $|f(x)| \ge 1$ for all $x$ with $|x| \le 1$;
(iii) $f$ is quadratic.

positive_text:
A number $n$ is called chill if it is a perfect square and there exist distinct positive integers $x$, $y$, $z$, $w$ such that $x > y$ and $z > w$, and $n = x^3 - y^3 + z^3 - w^3$. For example, $225$ is chill because $225 = 15^2$ and $225 = 7^3 - 5^3 + 2^3 - 1^3$. Show that:

a) $2025$ is chill;

b) there are infinitely many chill numbers.

negatives.text:
A number $n$ is called chill if it is a perfect square and there exist distinct positive integers $x$, $y$, $z$, $w$ such that $x > y$ and $z > w$, and $n = x^3 + y^3 + z^3 + w^3$. Show that:

a) $2025$ is chill;

b) there are infinitely many chill numbers.

A number $n$ is called chill if it is a perfect square and there exist distinct positive integers $x$, $y$, $z$, $w$ such that $x > y$ and $z > w$, and $n = x^2 - y^2 + z^2 - w^2$. Show that:

a) $2025$ is chill;

b) there are infinitely many chill numbers.

A number $n$ is called chill if it is a perfect square and there exist distinct positive integers $x$, $y$, $z$, $w$ such that $x > y$ and $z > w$, and $n = x^3 - y^3 + z^3 - w^3$, but all of $x, y, z, w$ must be even. Show that:

a) $2025$ is chill;

b) there are infinitely many chill numbers.

positive_text:
Let $(G, \cdot)$ be a group, and $K < G$ be a proper subgroup of $G$. Suppose there exist endomorphisms $\phi, \psi, \theta : G \to G$ such that $\phi(xy) = \psi(x)\theta(y)$ for all $x, y \in G \setminus K$. Prove that:

a) $\psi = \theta$;

b) if $G$ is nonabelian, and $K = Z(G)$, then $\phi = \psi = \theta$.

negatives.text:
Let $(G, \cdot)$ be a group, and $H < G$ be a proper subgroup of $G$. Suppose there exist homomorphisms $f, g, h : G \to G$ such that $f(xy) = g(x)h(y)$ for all $x, y \in H$. Prove that:

a) $g = h$;

b) if $G$ is nonabelian, and $H = Z(G)$, then $f = g = h$.

Let $(G, \cdot)$ be a group, and $H < G$ be a proper subgroup of $G$. Suppose there exist endomorphisms $f, g, h : G \to G$ such that $f(xy) = g(x)h(y)$ for all $x, y \in G \setminus H$. Prove that:

a) $g = h$;

b) if $G$ is abelian, and $H = Z(G)$, then $f = g = h$.

positive_text:
Find four prime numbers $p, q, r, s$, with $p \le q$ and $r \le s$, such that
(1) $p + q = r + s + 1$;
(2) $p^2 + q^2 + r^2 + s^2 = 3543$.

negatives.text:
Find four prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a \le b$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d - 1$;
(2) $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 3543$.

Find four prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a \le b$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d + 1$;
(2) $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 354$.

Find four prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a \le b$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d + 1$;
(2) $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 3543$;
(3) $a, b, c, d < 50$.

positive_text:
Let $X, Y \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ satisfy $X + Y = XY + YX$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(XY - YX) = 0$;
b) if $\det(X) \ne \det(Y)$, then $\det(XY - YX) = 0$.

negatives.text:
Let $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ satisfy $A + B = AB - BA$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) \ne \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

Let $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ satisfy $A^2 + B^2 = AB + BA$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) \ne \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

Let $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ satisfy $A + B = AB + BA$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is even, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) = \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

positive_text:
Let $n$ be the total number of different subjects offered at a university. For each positive integer $k \leq n$, define $A_{k}$ as the set of all students who have taken at least $k$ subjects; and let $B_{k}$ be the set of all students who, for any $k$ distinct subjects, have taken at least one of them. Prove that:

a. If $2k \geq n+1$, then $A_{k} \subseteq B_{k}$;

b. If $2k \leq n+1$, then $A_{k} \supseteq B_{k}$.

negatives.text:
Let $n$ be the total number of languages used in KAUST. For each positive integer $k \leq n$, let $A_{k}$ be the set of all people who can speak at least $k$ languages; and let $B_{k}$ be the set of all people who can speak all of any $k$ pairwise different languages. Prove that:

a. If $2k \geq n+1$, then $A_{k} \subseteq B_{k}$;

b. If $2k \leq n+1$, then $A_{k} \supseteq B_{k}$.

Let $n$ be the total number of languages used in KAUST. For each positive integer $k \leq n$, let $A_{k}$ be the set of all people who can speak exactly $k$ languages; and let $B_{k}$ be the set of all people who, for any $k$ pairwise different languages, can speak at least one of them. Prove that:

a. If $2k \geq n+1$, then $A_{k} \subseteq B_{k}$;

b. If $2k \leq n+1$, then $A_{k} \supseteq B_{k}$.

Let $n$ be the total number of languages used in KAUST. For each positive integer $k \leq n$, let $A_{k}$ be the set of all people who can speak at least $k$ languages; and let $B_{k}$ be the set of all people who can speak at least one of any $k$ languages they have encountered in the last year. Prove that:

a. If $2k \geq n+1$, then $A_{k} \subseteq B_{k}$;

b. If $2k \leq n+1$, then $A_{k} \supseteq B_{k}$.

positive_text:
Find all functions $g: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
1. $g(a+1) \geq g(a)+1$ for all $a \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $g(a b) \geq g(a) g(b)$ for all $a, b \in \mathbb{R}$.

negatives.text:
Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
1. $f(x+1) \geq f(x)+1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(x y) \leq f(x) f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
1. $f(x+1) > f(x)+1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(x y) \geq f(x) f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$.

positive_text:
Квадратна табела $n \times n$ попуњена бројевима $1, 2, \ldots, n^{2}$, сваки тачно једном, назива се нишка ако за сваку пермутацију $n$ елемената која изабира један елемент из сваке врсте и колоне, производ тих елемената има исту остатак при дељењу са $n^{2}+1$. Да ли постоји нишка табела за:

а. $n=8$;

б. $n=10$?

negatives.text:
Таблица димензија $n \times n$, попуњена бројевима $1, 2, \ldots, n^{2}$, назива се нишка ако сви збирови од по $n$ бројева који се налазе на $n$ „разбацаних" поља дају исти остатак при дељењу са $n^{2}+1$. Да ли постоји нишка таблица за:

а. $n=8$;

б. $n=10$?

Таблица димензија $n \times n$, попуњена бројевима $1, 2, \ldots, n^{2}$, назива се нишка ако сви производи од по $n$ бројева који се налазе на $n$ „разбацаних" поља дају исти остатак при дељењу са $n^{2}$. Да ли постоји нишка таблица за:

а. $n=8$;

б. $n=10$?

Таблица димензија $n \times n$, попуњена бројевима $1, 2, \ldots, n^{2}$, назива се нишка ако сви производи од по $n$ бројева који се налазе на $n$ „разбацаних" поља су једнаки. Да ли постоји нишка таблица за:

а. $n=8$;

б. $n=10$?

Let $n \ge 3$ be an integer, and consider a set of $n$ points in three-dimensional space such that:
(i) every two distinct points are connected by a string which is either red, green, blue, or yellow;
(ii) for every three distinct points, if the three strings between them are not all of the same colour, then they are of two different colours;
(iii) not all the strings have the same colour.
Find the maximum possible value of $n$.

Let $n \ge 3$ be an integer, and consider a set of $n$ points in three-dimensional space such that:
(i) every two distinct points are connected by a string which is either red, green, blue, or yellow;
(ii) for every three distinct points, if the three strings between them are not all of the same colour, then they are of at most three different colours;
(iii) not all the strings have the same colour.
Find the maximum possible value of $n$.

Let $n \ge 3$ be an integer, and consider a set of $n$ points in three-dimensional space such that:
(i) every two distinct points are connected by a string which is either red, green, or yellow;
(ii) for every three distinct points, if the three strings between them are not all of the same colour, then they are of three different colours;
(iii) not all the strings have the same colour.
Find the maximum possible value of $n$.

positive_text:
Für reelle Zahlen $p$, $q$, $r$, $s$ gelten die Gleichungen
$$
\begin{array}{ll}
p=\sqrt{45-\sqrt{21-p}}, & q=\sqrt{45+\sqrt{21-q}} \\
r=\sqrt{45-\sqrt{21+r}}, & s=\sqrt{45+\sqrt{21+s}}
\end{array}
$$
Zeige, dass gilt $p q r s=2004$.

negatives.text:
Für reelle Zahlen $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$ gelten die Gleichungen
$$
\begin{array}{ll}
a=\sqrt{45-\sqrt{21-a}}, & b=\sqrt{45+\sqrt{21-b}} \\
c=\sqrt{45-\sqrt{21+c}}, & d=\sqrt{45+\sqrt{21+d}}
\end{array}
$$
Zeige, dass gilt $a b c d=2000$.

positive_text:
令 $x, y, z, w$ 為非負實數, 求所有解 $(x, y, z, w)$ 滿足:
$$
x^2(y+z)(y+z+w) = \sqrt{y+z}\sqrt[3]{y+z+w}
$$
$$
y^2(z+w)(z+w+x) = \sqrt{z+w}\sqrt[3]{z+w+x}
$$
$$
z^2(w+x)(w+x+y) = \sqrt{w+x}\sqrt[3]{w+x+y}
$$
$$
w^2(x+y)(x+y+z) = \sqrt{x+y}\sqrt[3]{x+y+z}
$$

negatives.text:
已知 $a, b, c, d$ 為非負實數，試求所有解 $(a, b, c, d)$ 滿足以下條件：
$$
a^2(b+c)(b+c+d) = \sqrt{b+c}\sqrt[3]{c+d}
$$
$$
b^2(c+d)(c+d+a) = \sqrt{c+d}\sqrt[3]{d+a}
$$
$$
c^2(d+a)(d+a+b) = \sqrt{d+a}\sqrt[3]{a+b}
$$
$$
d^2(a+b)(a+b+c) = \sqrt{a+b}\sqrt[3]{b+c}
$$

positive_text:
